    "science_context": "The third known interstellar object. Twelve exposures over 3.3 hours on 14 December 2025 in detector D5 (about 3.8–4.4 µm, a range that includes the 4.26 µm CO₂ band) and six simultaneous D2 frames (about 1.1–1.65 µm). Every cutout has the same sky centre, so the stars stay fixed while the comet crosses about 0.16° (roughly 95 pixels) of the field. The marker shows IRSA’s predicted comet position for each exposure, not a measured position. IRSA warns of low signal-to-noise at many wavelengths, and the comet’s brightness changes with the wavelength at its position."
